course: biology
lesson: 405
title: The real animals of the Wild Wood: the badger, the stoat, the weasel, the ferret, field-mice and hedgehogs in winter
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In «The Wind in the Willows» the Wild Wood is full of stoats, weasels and ferrets who whistle, patter and finally steal Toad Hall, while the wise old Badger lives deep underground and the field-mice sing carols in the snow.
explain: Real stoats and weasels are not wicked: they are small hunters that must catch mice, voles and rabbits to live, just as a robin must catch worms. The book turns hunters into villains because that makes a good story.
explain: The Badger is closer to life: real badgers dig huge old homes called setts and stay underground through cold spells. But real badgers live in family groups, not alone, and they love company of their own kind.
explain: Some parts are simply wrong for the season: real hedgehogs are asleep all winter, so two little hedgehogs on their way to school in the snow could only happen in a story.
explain: So read the Wild Wood like a naturalist: enjoy the story, then ask what the real animal would be doing.
fact: Real badgers live in family groups of about five or six in a sett, a set of tunnels and chambers that can be used by badgers for a hundred years or more.
fact: Badgers do not truly hibernate, but in cold weather they stay underground for days, sleeping a lot and living on the fat they built up in autumn, a bit like the Badger who does nothing «strenuous» in winter.
fact: Badgers are very clean: they drag in fresh grass and bracken for bedding and dig special toilet pits away from the sett.
fact: Badgers are protected by law in Britain, so nobody may harm a badger or block or dig into a sett.
fact: A stoat and a weasel look alike, but a stoat is bigger and always has a black tip on its tail, while a weasel is smaller, with a short tail and no black tip.
fact: In cold northern places a stoat's coat turns white in winter, keeping only the black tail tip; this white fur is called ermine and was once used on kings' robes.
fact: A weasel is so small and thin that it can follow a mouse or vole right down its hole; a stoat is strong enough to catch a rabbit much bigger than itself.
fact: A ferret is a tame kind of polecat that people have kept for over two thousand years, and it was used to chase rabbits out of their burrows; many ferrets are pale cream or yellowish, like the book's «long yellow ferret».
fact: A wood mouse, also called the long-tailed field mouse, has big eyes and ears, stores seeds for winter and stays active all winter, so mice really could be out in the snow at Christmas.
fact: The harvest mouse is Britain's smallest rodent, about as heavy as a 20p coin, and it weaves a ball-shaped nest among tall grass or wheat stalks, holding on with its gripping tail; harvesting machines can destroy those nests, just as the book's field-mice feared.
fact: Hedgehogs hibernate from about November to March in a nest of leaves under a hedge or a shed; a small hedgehog out in the daytime in winter is usually in trouble, and an adult should call a wildlife rescue.
fact: Rabbits live in burrows called a warren and warn each other of danger by thumping the ground with their back feet and flashing their white tails as they run.
fact: The tawny owl's «twit twoo» is really two owls calling to each other: one calls «ke-wick» and the other answers «hoo-hoo-hoo».
fact: The nightjar, which frightened Toad in the night wood, is a bird that hunts moths at dusk, makes a long churring sound like a little engine and flies to Africa for the winter.
exercise: What is a badger's home called? | A sett | A holt | A drey | A warren
exercise: How do real badgers live? | In family groups of about five or six | Always alone, like the Badger in the book | In huge herds of hundreds out on open fields | In pairs that live in the tops of trees
exercise: What do badgers do in a long cold spell? | Stay underground, sleep a lot and live on their fat | Fly south to warmer countries for the winter | Hibernate without waking from October till May | Move into people's houses to find food and warmth
exercise: How do badgers keep their sett clean? | They bring in fresh bedding and dig toilet pits outside | They never sleep in the same chamber twice in one year | They let the winter rain wash right through all the tunnels | They make the rabbits next door tidy up for them
exercise: How can you tell a stoat from a weasel? | The stoat is bigger and has a black tail tip | The weasel is bigger and has a black tail tip | The stoat has stripes and the weasel has spots | Only the weasel has a tail at all
exercise: What is ermine? | A stoat's white winter fur | A weasel's summer nest | The black tip of a mole's tail | A special kind of badger hair
exercise: Why can a weasel hunt mice inside their holes? | It is small and thin enough to follow them in | It can dig faster than any other animal in Britain | It makes itself as flat as a leaf | It calls the mice out by squeaking
exercise: What large animal can a stoat catch? | A rabbit | A deer | A fox | A badger
exercise: What is a ferret? | A tame kind of polecat | A young stoat that has not grown its black tail tip yet | A kind of weasel that only lives in America | A kind of wild rat
exercise: What were ferrets often used for? | Chasing rabbits out of their burrows | Pulling the small carts of farmers | Catching fish in rivers and ponds | Guarding big country houses at night
exercise: What do wood mice do in winter? | Stay active and eat the seeds they stored | Sleep without waking from November to April | Fly away together to a warmer country | Turn white like a stoat and hide in the snow
exercise: Where does a harvest mouse build its nest? | A ball of grass woven among tall stalks | Deep down in a burrow under the roots of an oak tree | In a hole in a brick wall | In an empty bird's nest
exercise: How heavy is a harvest mouse? | About as heavy as a 20p coin | About as heavy as a bag of sugar | About as heavy as an apple | About as heavy as a pencil case
exercise: When do hedgehogs hibernate? | From about November to March | From about May to August | Only on the coldest night of the year | They never hibernate in Britain
exercise: How do rabbits warn each other of danger? | Thumping the ground with their back feet | Whistling loudly from the tops of the trees | Squeaking like mice deep under the ground | Clapping their two front paws together hard
exercise: What is a tawny owl's «twit twoo»? | Two owls calling to each other | One owl calling twice | A baby owl crying out for food | The owl's wings flapping as it flies
exercise: What does a nightjar eat? | Moths caught at dusk | Worms dug out of the soil | Seeds from the fields | Fish from the river
test: «weasels are evil, it says so in wind in the willows» What is a fair answer? | Real weasels are not evil; they are small hunters that must catch mice and voles to live | Yes, weasels are the only animals that know they are doing wrong and do it anyway | Yes, and that is exactly why weasels are hunted by law everywhere | Weasels are evil only in the Wild Wood, but kind in gardens
test: An animal with a black tip on its tail is running along a stone wall in Yorkshire. What is it most likely? | A stoat | A weasel | A ferret | A water vole
test: In the book Badger lives alone and «hates Society». How is a real badger different? | Real badgers live in family groups of about five or six in one sett | Real badgers live alone their whole life and never meet another badger, not even to breed | Real badgers live in towns in big crowds | Real badgers live with the rabbits in their warren
test: «found a big hole in the woods with fresh grass piled outside, can i dig it out and see?» What is right? | No, it may be a badger sett, and setts are protected by law | Yes, holes in the woods belong to nobody, so anyone may dig | Yes, badgers like visitors, especially in the daytime | Yes, but only with a small spade and very gently
test: In the book two little hedgehogs are walking to school through the snow. What would real hedgehogs be doing in the snow? | Hibernating in a nest of leaves | Looking for a school to go to | Hunting for snails under the snow | Building snowmen with the rabbits
test: «small hedgehog wandering about in the garden at lunchtime in january, so cute» What should you do? | Tell an adult so they can call a wildlife rescue, as it is probably in trouble | Leave it a big saucer of cow's milk and some bread, then go back inside | Pick it up and put it in your bedroom to sleep in a shoebox | Nothing, hedgehogs often go for long walks in the daytime in winter
test: In the book the field-mice hurry to move house «before those horrid machines begin clicking round the fields». Is that a real danger? | Yes, harvesting machines can wreck harvest mice's nests | No, mice never live anywhere near the wheat fields | No, harvest machines carefully drive round all the mice | No, mice live only in houses and barns
test: The book's field-mice go carol-singing in December. Which part could be true of real wood mice? | They are active in winter | They sing carols | They wear woollen scarves | They hibernate till spring
test: «that yellow ferret with a gun in the book, are ferrets wild in our woods then?» What is true? | Ferrets are tame polecats kept by people; any in a wood are escapes | Yes, ferrets are the commonest wild animal in British woods and parks | Yes, ferrets live in huge packs in every wood, like wolves | No, ferrets only live in the hot deserts of North Africa
test: You hear «ke-wick» and then «hoo-hoo-hoo» from the trees one night. What is going on? | Two tawny owls calling to each other | One owl calling twice, high then low | A nightjar copying an owl it has heard | A fox pretending to be an owl
test: ★★★ A stoat's coat is white for 4 months of the year in the far north and brown for the rest. A stoat lives 3 years. For how many months in all is it brown? | 24 | 12 | 32 | 36
exam: Identify the principal feature by which the stoat may be distinguished from the weasel. | The stoat is the larger and bears a black tip to its tail | The weasel is the larger and possesses a striped back and a bushy tail | The stoat bears spots upon its flanks | The weasel alone possesses whiskers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the social life of the European badger. | It lives in family groups occupying a sett that may be used for a century or more | It lives entirely alone and abandons its home each autumn in search of a new one | It lives in trees in pairs | It lives in burrows shared with rabbits
exam: Ascertain the action appropriate upon finding a small hedgehog abroad in daylight during January. | Inform an adult, so that a wildlife rescue may be consulted | Provide it with a quantity of cow's milk | Return it to the garden and take no further action whatever | Convey it indoors to sleep in a warm bed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ferret? | It is a tame form of the polecat, long kept for the hunting of rabbits | It is a wild animal native to every British wood and hedgerow, hunted by foxes | It is the young of the stoat before it grows its black tail tip | It is a species of rat
